course: dictionary
lesson: 8050
title: Regulators: keeping things in check
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A regulator is something that keeps another thing under control. It can be a device, like the part that keeps gas or water at a steady pressure, or the part that controls the speed of a clock. It can also be an official group that makes sure companies follow the rules.
explain: Use the context to find the meaning. «Regulators, official checkers, test a medicine before doctors can prescribe it.» The words official checkers are a clue: a regulator checks things. Ask yourself what job is being done, then test your meaning in the sentence to see if it fits.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, so read the whole sentence. «New medicines are tested in labs, then in careful trials with volunteers, and checked by regulators before doctors can prescribe them.» Here the word means a group of official checkers, not a machine. The sentence is about safety, so the checking is the clue.
fact: A regulator is a device that controls something, such as the pressure of gas or water, or the speed of a clock.
fact: A regulator can also be an official organisation that makes sure companies follow rules.
fact: Regulators can look after energy, water and TV.
fact: «Prescribe» means order, as in «doctors can prescribe it».
fact: «Side effects» means unwanted effects that come with a medicine.
exercise: «Regulators, official checkers, test a medicine before doctors can prescribe it.» What do regulators do here? | They make medicines for doctors to sell | They tell children what to eat | *They check that a medicine is safe before doctors can order it | They drive the doctors to work
exercise: What does a pressure regulator do? | It paints pictures of pressure | It stops the clock from ticking | It makes the pressure of gas or water steady | *It controls the pressure of gas or water
exercise: Find and copy one word from the sentence that means order. | trials | regulators | medicine | *prescribe
test: «New medicines are tested in labs, then in careful trials with volunteers, and checked by regulators before doctors can prescribe them.» Why are trials with volunteers needed? | *To find the best label colour | To make the medicine taste nicer | To give the volunteers a free holiday | To find out whether the medicine is safe to use
test: What is the meaning of «side effects» in the sentence about a medicine going on sale? | Happy results | Free samples | *Unwanted effects | Good news about the doctors
exam: Which of these is a regulator in the sense of an official organisation? | A valve that keeps water pressure steady | *A group that checks companies follow the rules | A pressure gauge on a car | A clock's speed control
